{
  "requirement": "Start an agent request that will decode its submitted answer as `R`. Its graph tools share the dependency list used by direct reads under {cite}`requirements.sdk.graph.validation-context.shared_context`.\n\n```rust,ignore\nimpl<'d, D: Domain> ValidationContext<'d, D> {\n    pub fn ask<R>(&self) -> Ask<'_, 'd, D, R, ContextPhase>\n    where\n        R: DeserializeOwned + JsonSchema + Send;\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The subject has two obligations. (1) `ask::<R>()` starts an agent request whose submitted answer is decoded as `R`. (2) The request's graph tools record reads in the same dependency list as the criterion's direct reads, per `shared_context`.\n\n**Obligation 2, tool reads shared with direct reads.** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/agent/configured.rs#mod[tests]/fn[verify_returns_direct_and_tool_reads_in_one_trace]` is marked `{test}` for `ask`. Its `ReadThenAsk` criterion does a direct `cx.read` of node 42, then calls `cx.ask::<bool>().await`. The backend script has the agent call the real `read_node` tool on node 0, which is node 7, and then call `submit_result`. The test asserts that the returned `answer.dependencies()` is exactly `[Node(42), Node(7)]`. So a direct read and a tool read land in one list, in order. The test also asserts that two backend requests were served. A version that kept tool reads in a separate list would fail this assertion.\n\n**Obligation 1, decoding as `R`.** The same test asks for `bool` and the agent submits `{\"result\": true}`, so the answer decodes to the requested type. `explicit_insertions_preserve_order_repetition_and_selection_snapshots` in `validation_tests.rs` calls `ctx.ask::<Answer>()`. It asserts `answer.n == 7` and that the schema sent to the agent has `properties.n.type == \"integer\"`. That shows both the decoding into `R` and the `JsonSchema` derivation. The `Send` bound is checked with `assert_send(&future)`.\n\n**Supporting tests.** `later_lighter_agent_calls_preserve_the_contexts_existing_trace` checks that a context's existing trace survives repeated `ask` calls. It also checks that `ask` works with no builder inserts. `public_exports_match_the_appendix` checks the exported signature and that the consumer compiles.\n\n**Remaining items.** The `DeserializeOwned + JsonSchema + Send` bounds and the `ContextPhase` return type are compile-time properties. The tests exercise them by compiling and using the builder. There are no `{fixme}` acknowledgments, and I found no gap.",
